course: dictionary
lesson: 5910
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and mark is one of them. The words around it are the clues that tell you which meaning is right. In «She got full marks for her spelling test», marks are scores. In «The workers spray coloured paint marks on the pavement», marks are signs.
explain: Ask yourself what the sentence is about: school work, a sign, or a game? Then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it fits. If it does not fit, try another meaning.
explain: Mark can also be a doing word. To mark homework means to check it and give it a score. To mark a day means to celebrate or remember it. The job a word does in a sentence can change its meaning.
fact: Mark can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (a doing word).
fact: «Mark» as a noun can mean a small sign, spot or line, or a score for school work.
fact: «Marks» in maths are the points you got; «out of 20» tells you the total that was possible.
fact: «Marked» means put a sign on something, or check and score work.
fact: «Marked separately» means two things are scored in different ways, so one does not change the other.
exercise: «She got full marks for her spelling test.» What are marks here? | Small spots on the paper | Games played outside | *Points or scores for school work | Lines on a map
exercise: «The coloured paint marks on the pavement show where the pipes run.» What does mark mean here? | To stay close to a player | *A sign or line on something | To celebrate a day | A score for school work
exercise: Teachers mark homework. What does mark mean here? | To hide it from the class | To celebrate it | *To check it and give it a score | To put a spot on it for fun
test: «Videos that are paid adverts are often marked "ad" or "sponsored", sometimes in small print.» What does marked mean here? | Celebrated with a party | *Labelled with a sign, so you can tell it is an advert | Hidden from view | Scored out of ten
test: «A brilliant story can come in messy letters, and the two are marked separately.» Why are they marked separately? | Because the teacher likes messy letters | *Because neat writing and good stories are different skills, so each gets its own score | Because a story is a kind of sign | Because they are both about paint
exam: «Some glasses have marks etched in the bottom to make a stream of bubbles.» What does marks mean here? | Scores for school work | *Small signs or lines cut into the surface | Days to remember | Players guarded in a game
